Give the device persistent crash capture.

There was none. /sys/fs/pstore mounted with no backend behind it and zero
entries, no ramoops, and no reset-reason register exposed, so a panic, oops or
watchdog reset left nothing at all to read afterwards. The logs that would say
why are the ones the unclean shutdown destroys - that is the whole point of
pstore, and it was not wired up.

That cost a real investigation: the device reset once without explanation and
the cause could not be established from the device at any price, only bounded
by reproducing the suspected trigger and failing to make it happen again.

CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM is already =m and ramoops.ko already ships, so nothing needs
building - what was missing is the reserved region for it to write into.

0x5c800000 is free system RAM: the bank runs 0x43030000-0x5f4fffff and the next
reservation above begins at 0x5c900000, so this takes the megabyte directly
below and stays contiguous with the existing carve-out rather than fragmenting
the middle of the bank.

The reg is FIXED rather than size/alloc-ranges as consys-pool uses, because
ramoops must find its records at the same physical address after the reset that
wrote them, and an allocator is free to place a dynamic region somewhere else
when the tree changes.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts
@@ -884,3 +884,29 @@
 	/delete-property/ alignment;
 	/delete-property/ alloc-ranges;
 };
+
+/*
+ * Crash records that survive a reset.
+ *
+ * This device had no persistent crash capture at all: /sys/fs/pstore mounted
+ * empty with no backend, so a panic, oops or watchdog reset left nothing to
+ * read afterwards - the logs that would say why are exactly the ones lost to
+ * the unclean shutdown that the reset causes. One unexplained reboot was
+ * investigated and closed as "cause unknown" purely for want of this.
+ *
+ * 0x5c800000 is free system RAM. The bank runs 0x43030000-0x5f4fffff and the
+ * next reservation above starts at 0x5c900000, so this takes the megabyte
+ * directly below it and stays contiguous with what is already carved out.
+ *
+ * A FIXED reg, not size/alloc-ranges as consys-pool uses: ramoops has to find
+ * its records at the same physical address after the reset that wrote them,
+ * and an allocator is free to choose differently when the tree changes.
+ */
+&{/reserved-memory} {
+	ramoops@5c800000 {
+		compatible = "ramoops";
+		reg = <0x0 0x5c800000 0x0 0x00100000>;
+		record-size = <0x00020000>;
+		ecc-size = <16>;
+	};
+};
